 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** $O(\log N)$ for traversal to the first hit, where $N$ is the number of voxels, due to the hierarchical structure. However, worst-case performance depends on the scene density and ray path.
-*   **Memory Complexity:** $O(N)$ for the octree nodes, but significantly less than a dense grid for sparse scenes. The DAG variant reduces this further [S7].
-*   **Performance:** Can render large scenes (e.g., >1 billion voxels) at real-time frame rates (e.g., 20 FPS at 720p) on modern GPUs [S6, unofficial].
-*   **Strengths:** Efficient empty space skipping, implicit LOD, single draw call rendering.
-*   **Limitations:** Dynamic updates are inefficient as the entire octree may need regeneration [S3, unofficial]. Memory constraints at high resolutions [S7].
+*   **Time Complexity:** $O(\log N)$ on average for sparse scenes, where $N$ is the number of voxels, as empty space is skipped. In the worst case (dense scene), it approaches $O(N)$ [S3, unofficial][S7].
+*   **Memory Complexity:** $O(M)$ where $M$ is the number of non-empty nodes. Significantly less than dense voxel grids [S7].
+*   **Performance:** Capable of rendering large scenes (e.g., >1 billion voxels) at real-time frame rates (e.g., 20 FPS at 720p) on modern GPUs [S6, unofficial].
+*   **Strengths:** Efficient empty space skipping, implicit LOD, single draw call rendering [S6, unofficial].
+*   **Limitations:** Dynamic updates are expensive (often requiring full reconstruction); memory overhead for node structures can be high at very high resolutions [S3, unofficial][S7].
 
 ## Background
-Sparse Voxel Octrees were introduced as a hierarchical data structure for raycasting, allowing for Level-of-Detail rendering by controlling traversal depth based on viewer distance [S3, unofficial]. The technique is used in various real-time rendering applications, including the rendering of large voxel scenes like the Stanford Dragon and complex tori clusters [S6, unofficial]. The method is often compared to DAG-based approaches which offer better compression [S7].
+Sparse Voxel Octrees were introduced as an efficient data structure for raycasting, building on the concept of octrees but omitting empty nodes to save memory [S3, unofficial]. The technique gained prominence with papers such as "Octree Textures on the GPU" (GPU Gems) and later optimizations by Laine and Karras [S2, S6, unofficial]. It is widely used in real-time voxel rendering engines and research projects like the Stanford Dragon renderer [S6, unofficial].
 
